PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H remains the clearest outlier here. The proposal that the blank circles represent a garbled transmission of Kuji-in hand seals re-coded as “nine holes,” Taoist erotic energy rituals, or yin-yang chakra amplification has no anchor in the Bansenshukai’s own language, in the Iga/Kōka corpus, or in any Edo-period administrative record. The scroll’s kunoichi passages are practical notes on disguise and household intelligence work, not ritual sex-magic; mapping the conventional copyist symbols ⬤○ onto bodily orifices or esoteric energy points is a modern overlay unsupported by period sources.  

By contrast, the remaining options, even the more speculative ones, stay within attested mechanisms: Confucian-minded scribes omitting material they found socially awkward, physical wear on heavily consulted folios, aburidashi techniques, oral mnemonics, or later institutional redaction. Those explanations may be stronger or weaker on the evidence, but they do not require inventing an entire erotic-esoteric framework that contradicts everything we know about how the text was composed and transmitted.  

Colleague 4’s point about Lady Saigō’s chronology is well taken and does weaken C, yet the same chronological and institutional constraints also make the centralized Oniwaban redaction in D harder to square with the 1676 date and the independent circulation of copies. H, however, is the only option that collapses under even modest scrutiny of the actual documentary record.}

8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H remains the least plausible by a clear margin. The proposal in H requires us to accept an entirely invented chain of esoteric mappings—Kujiho hand seals secretly encoding the “nine holes” of the female body, Taoist erotic rituals used to manipulate perception or energy flow, yin-yang chakra points, and so on—that appears nowhere in the Bansenshukai itself, in any other surviving Iga or Kōka manual, or in the administrative and scholarly records of the 1670s. The actual kunoichi passages preserved in every copy are terse and practical, dealing with disguise, intelligence collection, and occasional honey-trap work; they contain no ritual or sexual-alchemical content that would have needed such a coded substitution. The black and white circles are simply the standard Edo-period scribal convention for omitted or illegible characters, not a corrupted symbolic system.  

By contrast, every other option, however speculative, can be anchored to documented historical mechanisms: moral filtering by Confucian-minded copyists (B), physical wear on heavily consulted folios (G), the known use of aburidashi techniques (E), oral-transmission mnemonics (F), or later institutional interest in restricting operational tradecraft (D). Even the chronologically problematic political-suppression scenarios (A, C) at least invoke real actors and motives attested elsewhere in Tokugawa record-keeping. H alone demands an unsupported modern overlay of sexual esotericism that collapses against the textual and material evidence of how the scroll was actually copied and transmitted.  

Colleague 4’s timeline objections to C are well taken, yet they do not elevate H; they simply show that C, like several others, is imperfect but still operates within the realm of attested period practices rather than pure retrofitted fantasy.}

